===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|originally|US|derogatory}} [[favouritism|Favouritism]] to [[friend#Noun|friends]] without [[regard#Noun|regard]] for their [[qualification]]s; especially {{lb|en|politics}}, in their [[appointment]] to [[political#Adjective|political]] [[position#Noun|positions]].
#: {{synonyms|en|cronydom|wasta<q:Arab society>}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|nepotism|patronage}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Lawrence W. Pierce|Lawrence W[arren] Pierce]], United States District Judge|chapter=Memorandum Opinion and Order|title=John R. Patterson, et al., Plaintiffs, v Newspaper and Mail Deliverers’ Union of New York and Vicinity, et al., Defendants.{{nb...|Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, Plaintiff, v Newspaper and Mail Deliverers’ Union of New York and Vicinity, et al., Defendants. James Larkin, Dominick Ventre, Frank Chillemi, Gerald Katz, et al., Intervenors.}}|date=19 September 1974|page=23a|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IEkdlSp0C-wC&pg=RA2-PP99|oclc=|passage=[T]he present structure of the collective bargaining agreement, combined with nepotism and '''cronyism''' and other abuses in employment and referral practices, have perpetuated the effects of the past discrimination, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Norman H. Clark|chapter=Booze and Politics, 1933–83|title=The Dry Years: Prohibition and Social Change in Washington|edition=revised|location=Seattle, Wash.; London|publisher=w:University of Washington Press|year=1988|page=258|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=u9IhCwAAQBAJ&pg=PA258|isbn=978-0-295-96466-9|passage=[Robert L.] Hagist began what he called an "18 month reform program" that included revised warehouse procedures and measures to prevent the leakages, shortages, '''cronyisms''', and politicking attributed to the managers and clerks of the various stores.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Dick Armey, witness|title=Hearing on the Reauthorization of the National Endowment for the Arts and the Humanities: Hearings before the Subcommittee on Postsecondary Education of the [[w:United States House Committee on Education and the Workforce|Committee on Education and Labor]], [[w:United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]], One Hundred First [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], Second Session{{nb...|Hearings Held in Washington, DC, April 4 and May 2, 1990 [...] Printed for the Use of the Committee on Education and Labor}}|series=Serial No. 101-78|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=4 April 1990|volume=3|page=35|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=0rkgAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA35|oclc=21948605|passage=If we are going to reauthorize the National Endowment, we need to see to it that the Endowment, in its procedures, are open, one. {{...}} That there is a cessation of a long-term practice of '''cronyisms''' on the panels so that artists across the Nation have equal access to this.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Randall G. Holcombe|Randall G[regory] Holcombe]]; Andrea M. Castillo|chapter=Communism|title=Liberalism and Cronyism: Two Rival Political and Economic Systems|publisher=Mercatus Center at George Mason University|month=April|year=2013|page=31|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/liberalismcronyi0000holc/page/31/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-989193-0-3|passage=[C]ommunism is predicated upon a central structure of economic command that is singularly entrusted with allocating resources and making economic decisions. Because of this centralized command structure, communist societies fall prey to the forces of '''cronyism''' and influence-peddling as commune members without economic power curry favor with commune leaders that control access to resources.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas J. Gradel; [[w:Dick Simpson (politician)|Dick Simpson]]|chapter=Suburban Scandals|title=Corrupt Illinois: Patronage, Cronyism, and Criminality|location=Urbana&#59; Chicago, Ill.|publisher=w:University of Illinois Press|year=2015|page=117|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/corruptillinoisp0000grad/page/117/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-252-07855-2|passage=Patronage, nepotism, '''cronyism''', abuse of power, and criminal activity flourish, sometimes for decades, in numerous town halls, police stations, and special-purpose government agencies in the suburbs.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} The [[condition#Noun|condition]] of being friends; [[friendship]]; also, the [[ability]] or [[inclination]] to [[make#Verb|make]] friends.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Robert Taylor (Radical)|Robert Taylor]]|title=Characterism of Leeds|journal=The Lion|location=London|publisher={{w|Richard Carlile}},{{nb...|62, Fleet Street.}}|date=26 June 1829|volume=III|issue=26|page=813|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=RCVAAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA813|oclc=220722854|passage=[T]he preacher {{...}} was reading to them the duty of loving one another ''as Christians'', But only particularly and exclusively were they to love one another "''as Christians''," that is as confederates and caballers together in a particular interest, distinct from that of the great family of mankind; {{...}} The benefit to themselves from this Free-masonry sectarian '''''cronyism''''', (for which the uninitiated world is so much obliged to them) was, to be, that {{...}} [t]he spirit of God was to bear witness with their spirits, and to settle the matter of faith with a degree of conviction, that should render reason superfluous and inquiry unnecessary.}}
#* {{RQ:Hunt Seer|part=I|chapter=English and French Females. Their Costumes and Bearing.|column=1|page=73|passage=Our friend the Old Crony, we see, for all his connoisseurship and '''crony-ism''', his regard for a certain piquancy of perfection in the French dress and walk, and his wish that his fair countrywomen would "take steps" after their fashion, cannot get rid of the preference in which he was brought up for the beauty of the English countenance.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=“a mother” [pseudonym]|chapter=Peculiar Duties of Wives in Various Stations of Life|title=The Young Wife; or, Hints to Married Daughters.{{nb...|By a Mother.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Religious Tract Society|The Religious Tract Society]]|year=['''1841''']|page=123|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KP8CAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA2-PA129-IA4|oclc=1008481427|passage=She [the minister's wife] is warned against being drawn into excessive attachments, engrossing intimacies, low and gossiping '''cronyism''', all intermeddling with tales and talebearers, with family breaches, with partisanship, and with ecclesiastical jurisdiction.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:George Redford; w:John Angell James|chapter=Part II. Supplement to the Autobiography of the Rev. William Jay.|editors=George Redford; John Angell James|title=The Autobiography of the Rev. William Jay;{{nb...|with Reminiscences of Some Distinguished Contemporaries, Selections from His Correspondence, etc.}}|location=London|publisher=Hamilton, Adams, & Co.,{{nb...|33, Paternoster Row.}}|year=1854|page=223|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Wt1eAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA223|oclc=1517239|passage=[H]e &#91;[[w:William Jay (minister)|William Jay]]&#93; observed, that there were two things which caused religious servants to be too generally disliked. The ''first'' was their fondness for religious gossiping, or '''''cronyism''''', which made them regardless of their time, &c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Renton Nicholson|&#91;Renton Nicholson&#93;]]|chapter=XVIII|title=The Lord Chief Baron Nicholson. An Autobiography|location=London|publisher=George Vickers,{{nb...|Angel Court, Strand.}}|year=['''1860''']|page=183|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NaxcAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA183|oclc=251599856|passage=Pea-green Hayne was one of my most intimate friends; we occupied rooms ''vis à vis'', and made prison life more than endurable by affectionate '''cronyism'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Mortimer Collins|chapter=Double Entanglement|title=The Ivory Gate.{{nb...|[...] In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher={{w|Hurst and Blackett}},{{nb...|publishers, 13, Great Marlborough Street.}}|year=1869|volume=I|page=222|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cNkBAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA222|oclc=17395831|passage=And the Englishman and Irishman went off together in a state of thorough '''cronyism''', the former imagining that he had quite taken in his companion, and entertaining no suspicion that it was rather the other way.}}

